#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# position

#% text
# `position(t, p)` gives the positions of the parts of `t` that match the pattern `p`, as index lists for `part`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Parts at every depth are searched, the whole term and heads included; each position is a list of indices.

#% item
# Positions come in the order the parts are written.

#% item
# `t[pos...]`, or `part(t, i, j, ...)`, gets the part back.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
position([a, b, a, c], a)
#% output
#= [[1], [3]]

#% cell
position([[1, 2], [3, 1]], 1)
#% output
#= [[1, 1], [2, 2]]

#% cell
position([1, x, 2.5, y], _: Int)
#% output
#= [[1]]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Positions inside an expression:

#% cell
position(f(x, g(x, y)), x)
#% output
#= [[1], [2, 1]]

#% text
# Only the parts at level 2:

#% cell
position([a, [a, b]], a, [2])
#% output
#= [[2, 1]]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Where the largest value of a list occurs:

#% cell
v = [3, 9, 2, 9, 4]
position(v, max(v))
#% output
#= [[2], [4]]

#% text
# The entry of a matrix found by its value, then read back:

#% cell
m = [[4, 7], [7, 1]]
p = position(m, 1)
m[p[1][1], p[1][2]]
#% output
#= 1

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The number of positions is the number of matches at the top level for a flat list:

#% cell
length(position([a, b, a, c], a)) == count([a, b, a, c], a)
#% output
#= true

#% text
# `ordering` gives the positions that sort a list:

#% cell
ordering([30, 10, 20])
#% output
#= [2, 3, 1]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The search goes into every part, so a pattern may match inside elements as well:

#% cell
position([1, [1, 2]], 1)
#% output
#= [[1], [2, 1]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `part`, `cases`, `count`, `ordering`, `select_first`
